cff-version: 1.2.0
message: 'To cite package "locationallocation" in publications use:'
type: software
license: GPL-3.0-or-later
title: 'locationallocation: Maximal Coverage Location-Allocation Using Geospatial
  Data'
version: 0.2.0.9000
doi: 10.31223/X5XQ69
identifiers:
- type: url
  value: https://github.com/giacfalk/locationallocation/
abstract: Tools to solve Maximal Coverage Location-Allocation problems with geospatial
  data, i.e., to place new facilities so that as much (optionally weighted) demand
  as possible can reach a facility within a given travel time. Travel times are computed
  over the friction surfaces of the Malaria Atlas Project (Weiss et al., 2018 <https://doi.org/10.1038/nature25181>),
  optionally refined with OpenStreetMap road networks. New facilities can be placed
  anywhere within an area (continuous problem) or selected among candidate locations
  (discrete problem), using greedy (Church and ReVelle, 1974 <https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01942293>)
  and vertex substitution (Teitz and Bart, 1968 <https://doi.org/10.1287/opre.16.5.955>)
  heuristics. It is designed to help decision-makers allocate resources and facilities
  in contexts such as public health, emergency response, climate adaptation, and urban
  planning.
